I don't think so, as that places specificity on the generic article. {le
djine} would most certainly mean they both give her the same ring, but {lo
djine} means "one or more things which actually are a ring or torus". If
John gives her a donut, and Jim gives her an inner tube, then the statement
{la .djan. e la .djim. dunda lo djine la .meris.} is still true.


